 a metadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4155655\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nDOMAIN: ibtimes.sg\nSLUG: bitcoin-falls-below-66000-1-8-billion-crypto-liquidation-wave-hits-markets-87293\nTEXT: Bitcoin\'s price fell below $66,000 as a crypto liquidation wave hit the markets. CoinGlass reported an increase in Bitcoin open interest from 759,000 BTC to 788,600 BTC during the decline. Analysts interpreted this as evidence of growing short interest, with traders betting on further downside despite leveraged longs being flushed out. Retail traders remained largely net long, while large-account positioning shifted more bearish on Binance, OKX, and Bybit. A sustained break below $65,000 could increase the risk of a move toward $60,000, while stabilization above this level could support a short-term relief rally.\n\nID: 4156101\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nDOMAIN: coindesk.com\nSLUG: bitcoin-s-fear-gauge-surges-nearly-20-its-biggest-jump-since-feb-5-crash\nTEXT: Bitcoin\'s fear gauge, a measure of market sentiment, surged nearly 20% in its biggest jump since the February 5 crash. The slide to $66,000 is accelerating a shift into digital dollars as experts remain cautious about the cryptocurrency market. The UK\'s financial watchdog has cracked down on Premier League crypto partnerships, citing concerns over regulatory oversight. Executives say DeFi won\'t win over big banks until it fixes its hacking problem, which has led to a loss of $1.6 billion in bullish crypto bets as ETH, SOL, and DOGE drop 9%.\n\nID: 4156094\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nDOMAIN: coindesk.com\nSLUG: bitcoin-s-slide-to-usd67-000-is-accelerating-a-shift-into-digital-dollars\nTEXT: Bitcoin\'s slide to $67,000 is accelerating a shift into digital dollars as the cryptocurrency market faces significant challenges. The recent 9.5% drop in seven days has experts remaining cautious, with some predicting further selloffs. In contrast, stablecoin yields are attracting attention from banks, with a survey revealing people\'s reluctance to rock the boat on lending. Prediction market traders are also betting that bitcoin\'s selloff has further to run, with bullish crypto bets losing $1.6 billion as ETH and SOL drop 9%.\n\nID: 4156099\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nDOMAIN: coindesk.com\nSLUG: bitcoin-hits-power-law-level-low-that-historically-precedes-a-rebound\nTEXT: Bitcoin has hit a historically low level, similar to 2015 and 2016, which precedes a rebound. The cryptocurrency has been sliding 9.5% in seven days and is currently trading at $67,000. Experts remain cautious about the market\'s future, with some predicting that the selloff has further to run. Prediction market traders are also betting on a continued decline, with Bitcoin\'s selloff expected to accelerate.\n\nID: 4156100\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nDOMAIN: coindesk.com\nSLUG: bitcoin-plunges-below-usd66-000-even-as-global-stocks-hit-fresh-records\nTEXT: Bitcoin has plunged below $66,000, a significant drop of 9.5% in seven days, despite global stocks hitting fresh records. Experts remain cautious, warning that DeFi won\'t win over big banks until it fixes its hacking problem. In contrast, bullish crypto bets have lost $1.6 billion as ETH, SOL, and DOGE drop 9%. The slide to $66,000 is accelerating a shift into digital dollars.\n\nID: 4156103\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nDOMAIN: coindesk.com\nSLUG: bitcoin-steadies-at-usd67-000-faces-critical-juncture-after-sliding-9-5-in-seven-days\nTEXT: Bitcoin has steadied at $67,000 after a 9.5% decline in seven days, but faces a critical juncture. The cryptocurrency\'s 0.7% recovery on Wednesday did little to mask the decline, leaving it back in a range that persisted between February and April. If bitcoin tumbles below $60,000, it could trigger a wave of liquidations and potentially slide to as low as $54,000.\n\n\n',)
